WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.533
Former President

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:01.101
Donald Trump

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.403
is facing a new test of his 2024

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:04.571
presidential campaign

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:06.006
on the eve of his remarks

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.108
to an influential conservative group.

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:09.376
Some of Trump's competitors

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:11.011
made their appeals today.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.480
CNN's Jeff Zeleny has more on C-PAC

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.848
and the Republican divisions

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.649
on display there.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.419
It's long been a command performance

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:20.320
for Republicans

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.355
harboring White House ambitions.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:25.158
It's great to be back at C-PAC,

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:26.459
but at the annual Conservative

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:27.761
Political Action Conference

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:28.862
outside Washington,

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.697
the parade of potential presidential

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.866
hopefuls is far shorter this year.

00:00:32.932 --> 00:00:34.000
Former South Carolina

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.268
Governor Nikki Haley.

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:37.170
If you're tired and losing

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.572
puts your trust in a new generation,

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:40.940
and if you want to win,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.610
not just as a party, but as a country,

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.478
then stand with me.

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:46.046
And former

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:46.980
Secretary of State

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.947
Mike Pompeo

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.849
gently called for a new direction

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.151
We shouldn't look for larger than life

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:52.318
personalities, but rather

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.421
we should find power in the rooms

00:00:54.654 --> 00:00:55.422
like this one.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:56.322
But the long running,

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.258
three day gathering called CPAC

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:00.326
is now seen as the Trump show.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.429
These are my people. This is beautiful.

00:01:02.429 --> 00:01:04.264
The former president is set to appear

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:05.031
Saturday,

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:06.866
joining a sea of loyal supporters

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.468
and members of his own family.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:09.869
Your president, President

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:11.371
Donald Trump, will be here

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.640
who are rallying to return him to office.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:15.442
But other big name contenders

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:16.576
who many Republicans see

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.112
as the party's future had other plans

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:21.481
Last year,

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:22.315
Florida Governor Ron

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:23.817
DeSantis took to the stage

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:26.286
as a rising star, CPAC.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:27.620
But as he inched closer

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.289
to declaring a presidential bid,

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:30.924
he attended a gathering of donors

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:31.591
in Florida

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.126
hosted by Club for Growth,

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:34.394
an anti-tax group

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.496
urging the party to move on from Trump.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:37.897
Several potential rivals

00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.100
also skipped C-PAC and headed to Florida,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.734
including former Vice President

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:42.635
Mike Pence.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:44.671
South Carolina Senator Tim Scott.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:46.473
South Dakota Governor Kristi Noem.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.541
And New Hampshire Governor Chris Sununu.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.511
And but admiration for Trump

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.480
was on full display at CPAC,

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.013
where Eddie

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.748
Phillips took a seat at a replica

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:56.883
Resolute desk

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:59.552
against a backdrop of a faux Oval Office.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:01.054
Trump first. Okay.

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:03.890
And then DeSantis let's do that in 2018.

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:04.591
Colleen

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:06.459
Hoffman is from Jacksonville, Florida.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.061
She wore a DeSantis hat

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:10.263
even as she sported a Trump sticker.

00:02:10.263 --> 00:02:12.031
She said she's torn but believes

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.501
Trump is the stronger choice for 2024.

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:16.636
I really love this hat because it's like,

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:17.570
leave us alone.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:18.571
You know, it's I love it.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:19.439
But as of right now, I'm

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:20.306
looking for downtown

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.075
at the early stage of the campaign.

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.144
It's hardly a two man contest.

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.279
As Vivek Ramaswamy, an Ohio

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:27.881
businessman who jumped into the race

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:30.450
last month, made clear on the CPAC stage

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.584
when we rallied

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:32.919
behind the cry

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.555
to make America Great again,

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:38.591
we did not just hunger for a single man

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:42.028
We hungered for the unapologetic pursuit

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:43.396
of excellence.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:45.465
That is what it means to be an American.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:47.100
Yet even as the Republican field

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.402
grows, the conversations at CPAC

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.437
and the comparisons between candidates

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.039
always came back to Trump.

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:54.841
I think Governor DeSantis is wonderful.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:56.042
I think he's amazing.

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:57.610
I just don't think it's his time

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.178
quite yet.

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.512
I think if he could just give it

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:00.480
four years,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.381
I think he'd be a great

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:02.148
successor to Trump.

00:03:05.485 --> 00:03:06.419
So in the middle of all

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.087
the celebration of the former

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.455
President Donald Trump,

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:10.657
these gentle calls

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:11.958
to turn to

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.593
a new generation are not met

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:15.195
with a lot of applause here

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:16.196
today, Brianna.

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:16.563
Clearly,

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:19.165
there is a respect for these candidates,

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.100
but there is a love for Donald Trump.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.235
So when he arrives here

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:24.504
tomorrow, the question what does he do?

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.105
Does he start distinguishing himself

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:27.473
from some of his rivals?

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:29.842
His aides say count on it.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:31.211
Yeah, it's going to be tough.

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:33.313
This really shows us the divide there.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:34.514
Jeff, stay with us, if you would.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:35.415
I want to bring in CNN's

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:37.750
Audie Cornish to the conversation.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.786
Audie, I mean, we look at C-PAC.

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:40.520
How much does

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:41.321
this illustrate

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:42.322
the challenge ahead

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:44.290
for any possible Trump challenger?

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:45.225
Well, I think as CPAC

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:46.593
has become a home for more

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:48.161
and more kind of right wing voices

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:49.262
and at this point is

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.264
sort of fully ideologically captured

00:03:51.264 --> 00:03:53.199
by the sort of Trump

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.201
family and Trump politics.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:56.903
This is where you do

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.671
kind of have a testing ground

00:03:58.671 --> 00:03:59.973
for these messages.

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:01.140
I don't think it's an accident

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:01.541
that Nikki

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:04.844
Haley is there trying out her argument.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:06.145
Unfortunately, her argument

00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.313
to a roomful of people is

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.482
that Republicans have been losing

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:11.484
and now it's time to be a winner.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:12.285
I mean, you don't say that

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:12.952
to a bunch of people

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.721
who probably believe

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:17.390
President Trump's kind of election lies.

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:18.925
So there's still this

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:21.294
kind of it's not clear what

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:22.695
that message is.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.264
If you are an alternative

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:24.664
to Trump,

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.065
how to make that message

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:28.768
safely to his base of supporters.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:29.369
Yeah.

00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:29.969
And Jeff,

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:31.671
you're talking to voters here at CPAC.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:32.705
What are they saying to you?

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.841
Should be required to take a medal.

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:37.744
Well, this is a sea of true believers.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:41.314
I mean, the merchandise on display every

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.616
type of Trump.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:45.084
45 of course

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:46.653
he was the 45th president trying to be

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.221
the 47th is on display.

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:49.355
So this is

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.256
this is his crowd

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:50.823
but if you talk

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:51.991
a little bit deeper to them,

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:53.493
there is a sense of,

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.328
you know, is he the future of the party?

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:56.863
That, of course, is his burden to show

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:57.463
that he can be

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:59.132
the future of the party as well.

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:00.566
But one thing that is so different

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:01.601
at this is CPAC

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:02.201
and we've covered

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.503
so many of them is

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:04.470
when there's a Democrat

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.471
in the White House,

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:06.306
this is the year,

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:06.572
the year

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.374
before the presidential election

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:09.175
that is supposed to be

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:10.376
the testing ground

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:11.144
the proving ground

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.712
for Republican candidates

00:05:12.712 --> 00:05:14.781
to talk about conservative policies,

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.049
conservative ideas,

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:16.816
much less

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.985
talk about conservative policies,

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:19.519
the debt

00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:20.320
and other things

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.255
then a cult of personality

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:23.423
around the former president.

00:05:23.423 --> 00:05:25.591
That's why this event is so different.

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:28.328
Certainly is not the harbinger of

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:30.596
of what's to come in the broader

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:32.065
sense of the Republican primary.

00:05:32.065 --> 00:05:33.933
This is just one sort of stop along

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.401
the way. The actual

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:36.936
in a real

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:38.404
Republican voters and others out

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:40.606
there will certainly make this decision

00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:42.542
But this is a essentially a Trump

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.211
rally spread over three days in a hotel

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:47.113
here just outside Washington.

00:05:47.113 --> 00:05:48.548
So not necessarily

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:49.782
a broader sample

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:51.884
of what some Republicans are looking for.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:52.618
Yeah, Audie.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:53.820
And you make such a good point, Audie,

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.521
when you say that

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:56.656
Nikki Haley is saying

00:05:56.656 --> 00:05:57.490
to a group of people

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:58.858
who may think that Trump won,

00:05:58.858 --> 00:05:59.992
hey, if you're tired of losing,

00:05:59.992 --> 00:06:00.960
they don't think that

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.528
he lost necessarily,

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.297
but she still has to convince them

00:06:04.297 --> 00:06:05.698
that she can win.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:06.866
And I wonder

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:09.268
if she has a winning lane here

00:06:09.302 --> 00:06:11.070
considering this divide.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.171
And another question is,

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:13.172
how many other people

00:06:13.172 --> 00:06:14.741
are going to be in that lane?

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:15.808
I mean, technically, Mike

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.144
Pompeo would be in that lane.

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:19.045
Even the former vice

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:21.280
president Pence would be in that lane.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.116
The alternative to Trump Lane

00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:23.716
that maybe

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:27.253
is still kind of aligned or connected to

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.588
what would be considered

00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:30.723
kind of establishment Republican values

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:31.591
or people

00:06:31.591 --> 00:06:32.692
who are making the argument

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:33.826
that they could perform

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:36.095
much better in a general election.

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:38.064
I think that's still a hard sell

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:39.298
in these early days.

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:40.767
However, I want to add one other thing.

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:43.002
The fact that so many other potentials

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:43.603
are sort

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:44.871
of courting the donors,

00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:47.640
courting the Club for Growth crowd

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.475
means that they do see some sort

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:50.977
of other path

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:52.779
for support within the party.

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.180
And it will be interesting to see

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.516
who comes out of that money race,

00:06:56.516 --> 00:06:57.917
so to speak.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.118
In a couple of months.

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:00.920
Yeah, it's a crowded field, Jeff.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.488
It's very crowded.

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.690
It will be is our expectation.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:04.924
It

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:07.326
It is.

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:07.560
And of

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.094
course, every

00:07:08.094 --> 00:07:09.095
new face,

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.129
the larger the field,

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:10.496
the more it

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:11.831
benefits the former president.

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:12.532
No doubt about it.

00:07:12.532 --> 00:07:15.301
That's how he came to win the nomination

00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:16.903
back in 2016

00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:19.672
after that very large 2015 primary fund.

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:20.373
So that

00:07:20.373 --> 00:07:22.341
actually is the bigger point here.

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:24.177
The donor event down in Florida

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:26.145
some donors are trying to get behind

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:27.914
Governor Santos or others

00:07:27.914 --> 00:07:29.615
trying to keep this field small.

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:30.683
That is going to be the thing

00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:31.551
to keep an eye on that

00:07:31.551 --> 00:07:32.718
we'll be talking about

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:33.486
for the days to come.

00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:34.454
How many candidates

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:36.355
will be on that debate stage

00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.524
in August at the first Republican debate.

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:39.992
A crowded field

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:42.328
certainly benefits the former president.

00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:43.129
Sure does.

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:45.131
Jeff and Audie, thank you so much.

00:07:45.131 --> 00:07:46.466
And be sure to catch Audi's

00:07:46.466 --> 00:07:47.934
podcast, the assignment

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:49.202
with Audie Cornish,

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:52.004
available wherever you get your podcasts.

